            <ArticleTitle>Post-thoracotomy Horner syndrome associated with extrapleural infusion of local anesthetic.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>309-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1510/icvts.2008.198085</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Continuous incisional infusion of local anesthetic through an extrapleural catheter to achieve an intercostal nerve block is a safe and effective adjunct to control postoperative pain after thoracotomy. Local and systemic complications are rare. Here we present a case of an acute, reversible, post-thoracotomy Horner syndrome associated with the use of local anesthetic infusion via an intraoperatively placed extrapleural catheter.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
